00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.380
When it comes to moving, ciliates are spectacular
to watch.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:31.380
Their hair-like cilia beat in waves that steer
them around the microcosmos, and that’s

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:35.680
great if you’re trying to get from Point
A to Point B. But sometimes you’re not actually

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.160
trying to get anywhere.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:42.150
You’re just trying to be slightly less in
the spot you are currently in.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:47.890
Like these spirostomum, which can grow several
millimeters in length.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:53.260
That size can be an advantage when they’re
looking for food or navigating the world around

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.820
them, but it also makes them easy targets
for predators.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.899
Same for the vorticella, which anchor themselves
to substrates and then extend their feeding

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:06.229
heads out into the microcosmos.

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:10.880
It makes them kind of like fishermen, waiting
for food to come by, but it also makes them

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.149
sitting ducks for predators.

00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:18.840
But spirostomum, vorticella, and many other
ciliates do have a way of defending themselves

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:28.979
from these attacks: contracting.

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:32.929
Contracting is like very, very effective flinching.

00:01:32.929 --> 00:01:37.639
If you accidentally touch a hot burner on
the stove, you flinch and pull your hand away

00:01:37.639 --> 00:01:39.090
very quickly.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:43.630
Now imagine if you could not only pull your
hand back, but you could actually shrink your

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:49.549
hand down into your arm to minimize the threat
of the stove even further.

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:54.829
Well, the microcosmos does not have stoves
(though, I guess it could be said that stoves

00:01:54.829 --> 00:01:58.759
have a microcosmos—but that’s a story
for a different day).

00:01:58.759 --> 00:02:04.689
Instead, microbes are caught up in a great
game of Marco Polo with predators, responding

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:11.129
to chemical and physical cues to detect possible
threats, and responding in their own ways.

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:16.750
Of course, there’s the tried and true chemical
defense, which spirostomum do use.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:22.400
Scientists have documented a predator vomiting
up a spirostomum whose toxins didn’t digest

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.160
very well.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.819
But while toxins are a great defensive option
to have, if you’re a spirostomum or any

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:35.569
other ciliate, you probably don’t want to
get to the point where you need to be vomited up.

00:02:35.569 --> 00:02:40.620
Plus, even if you’ve managed to evade the
same predator again and again like spirostomum

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:46.480
has, there’s plenty of other obstacles to
avoid in the microcosmos.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.189
Like poop.

00:02:48.189 --> 00:02:54.819
I mean yeah, when you have video of Spirostomum
getting pooped on you have to use it.

00:02:54.819 --> 00:02:55.819
Anyway...

00:02:55.819 --> 00:03:00.549
Whether you are avoiding predators or their
poop, contracting is a good response to have.

00:03:00.549 --> 00:03:03.180
And these contractions are marvelous.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:08.909
They are some of the fastest things in nature
and not only do the contractions happen quickly,

00:03:08.909 --> 00:03:15.719
but the time between when the stimulus is
received and the contraction begins is faster

00:03:15.719 --> 00:03:25.939
than any action you have ever taken.

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:30.400
Scientists have been observing and documenting
different ciliates and their contractile behaviors

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:35.469
for decades now, and it’s not hard to understand
their fascination.

00:03:35.469 --> 00:03:41.439
For one, the contraction looks a bit like
the movement of our own muscles, just much

00:03:41.439 --> 00:03:42.799
much faster.

00:03:42.799 --> 00:03:46.069
Though it turns out it works much differently.

00:03:46.069 --> 00:03:49.060
And it’s also just very easy to get a ciliate
to contract.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:53.620
And that turns out to be an important thing
when you want to study something.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.849
Stentors have been a popular contractile ciliate
model because all you have to do to get them

00:03:57.849 --> 00:04:02.620
from their distinctive trumpet shape to a
sphere is poke them with a glass needle.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:06.849
Now, it’s important to note here that we’re
using this word “contraction” like it’s

00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:11.659
a uniform behavior done in the same way in
all ciliates.

00:04:11.659 --> 00:04:16.780
But just like how animals have a lot of different
ways to run around, ciliates have a lot of

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:18.740
different ways to contract.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:24.920
Vorticella, for example don’t contract their
whole bodies, they spiralize their stalks,

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:29.920
which coil them towards whatever they’re
attached to, and away from whatever stimuli

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:31.480
has set them off.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.990
And spirostomum have their own twist—a literal
one.

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:40.880
Specialized membrane structures and a long
coil of microtubles spin their ends, while

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.560
keeping their middle stable to activate contraction
in an ultra-fast movement that was only really

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.270
deeply understood in 2019.

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:54.600
Now again, whatever the change is for the
organism though, one of the things these species

00:04:54.600 --> 00:05:00.700
share in common is that the contractions are
very, very fast—so much so that it’s taken

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:05.550
high speed cinematography for scientists to
start to figure out how it works.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:11.260
Stentors, spirostomum, and vorticella all
condense to fractions of their original length

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:12.920
in a matter of milliseconds.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:18.420
A contracting spirostomum can experience forces
of around 14g.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:24.470
For comparison, an elite fighter pilot doesn’t
ever experience more than 9 gs of force.

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:29.920
And for vorticella, the rapid contraction
of their stalk moves them at about 1200 body

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:31.610
lengths per second.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:33.790
That is very fast.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:38.540
That’s the equivalent of a cheetah running
a mile in a second.

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:43.380
Now interestingly, while the organisms we’re
highlighting today work on the scale of milliseconds

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:50.190
to contract, they are much slower on the recovery,
taking more on the order of seconds to extend

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:51.890
back to their original state.

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:57.260
Now this makes sense, as the goal is to do
whatever possible to escape being eaten, so

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:03.360
the initial quick movement is more important
than the return to our normal activities movement.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:11.570
But the factors driving this fast contraction
and slow relaxation are both physical and chemical.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:16.240
Now physically, the bodies of these organisms
contain a mix of contractile fibers that work

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.320
together and against each other to create
these movements.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.680
In stentors and spirostomum, you can see these
special membrane structures running along

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:26.970
their bodies.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:32.740
And in the vorticella stalk, you can see this
helical, contracting organelle called the

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:33.810
spasmoneme.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:39.680
Whatever these things look like, you can think
of any of these movements not as sudden contractions

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:47.780
of muscle fibers, but instead, as chemicals
that change shape in different chemical conditions.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:53.190
If the solution they are in has a bunch of
calcium ions, the structures are small and

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:59.390
tightly coiled, if the solution has a low
concentration of calcium ions, they stretch out.

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:06.060
And so, in all of these cases, mechanical
stimulation causes these cells to suddenly

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:14.150
and rapidly allow calcium ions into the cell,
which causes all these structures to dramatically

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:16.510
and rapidly change shape.

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:22.690
But in order to return to the original shape,
the calcium ions have to be slowly and carefully

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:24.860
pumped out of the cytoplasm.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:32.110
So, calcium ions are used across all of biology
to carry information and cause changes in

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:38.740
all kinds of biological systems, including
the biological system that is you.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:43.410
But this reliance on calcium ions is kind
of strange.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:45.290
It’s very common.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:51.010
But the evolution of these systems relying
on calcium is a little weird because calcium

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:56.720
ions can interfere with reactions that are
key to our cellular metabolism.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.850
One recent theory to try and reconcile this
contradiction is that the rapid, calcium-driven

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:09.770
responses we are looking at right now are
part of a much longer evolutionary arc—one

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:15.770
built on the need for ancient cells to repair
damage to their membranes in order to prevent

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:19.900
a fatal influx of calcium ions from their
surroundings.

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:25.350
The same urgency that could have driven the
evolution of this biological first aid kit

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:30.150
might have then been rewired for other rapid
sensors.

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:35.880
And if this theory is correct, it means that
nature converted a defense against a chemical

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:42.510
into a signaling mechanism that drives everything
from the contractions you are watching, to

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:49.720
the action potentials that are helping you
understand the words that you are hearing right now.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:57.900
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

